<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Impact of Continued Pre-training</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To measure the importance of the continued pre-training phase, we evaluate its impact on the training efficiency and retrieval performance of the end models for generative retrieval.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1. </span>Experiment Setup</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our training methodology contains two stages: continued pre-training and generative retrieval fine-tuning, each tailored with a specific learning objective and a corresponding set of hyperparameters. To evaluate the distinct contributions of each step, we design a controlled 2x2 ablation study. This involves four model configurations that share the same decoder-only Transformer architecture. We use the MoE-900M architecture for this study. The four models are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R1: retrieval SFT (random init).</span> Transformer model is directly trained on the generative retrieval task with randomly initialized weights.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R2: retrieval SFT (LLM init).</span> This model is initialized from a pre-trained LLM checkpoint, but bypasses the CPT stage.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CR1: CPT (random init) + retrieval SFT.</span> The model training follows the two PLUM training stages, but the model used in CPT is randomly initialized.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CR2: CPT (LLM init) + retrieval SFT.</span> This represents the full and intended implementation of our proposed framework.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2. </span>Experiment Results</h4>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.07784v1/cpt-ablation-recall.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:234/128;" width="234" height="128" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.07784v1/cpt-ablation-loss.png" id="S3.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:234/128;" width="234" height="128" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">8-th Day Recall@10 and training loss vs retrieval SFT training step.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Impact of Continued Pre-training.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main results are summarized in Table <a href="#S3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Impact of Continued Pre-training. ‣ 3.3.2. Experiment Results ‣ 3.3. Impact of Continued Pre-training ‣ 3. Experiments ‣ PLUM: Adapting Pre-trained Language Models for Industrial-scale Generative Recommendations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Following the scaling study, the performance of each of the four models is evaluated on a large, held-out next day dataset, and we report recall@10 as the retrieval metric. There is a large performance gap between R1 and CR1 (or R2 and CR2), showing the benefit of CPT for retrieval fine-tuning. Furthermore, Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3.2. Experiment Results ‣ 3.3. Impact of Continued Pre-training ‣ 3. Experiments ‣ PLUM: Adapting Pre-trained Language Models for Industrial-scale Generative Recommendations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the 8-th day recall@10 and the training loss against training steps. It’s clear to see that the models with CPT can converge much faster. Therefore, CPT can benefit the training efficiency of task-specific fine-tuning, especially when multiple downstream tasks share the same CPT model.</p>
</div>
